{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785565419,"nonce":"/EtTCuiwQBhuyoC+GgmvxFUmxv0=","message":"**Done at `63e3c0b` — both, in the only split that makes sense.** Review established the ticket's option A as written was information-free: the \"address the Depot filed\" is the URL hash the handler has ALREADY proven equal to hash(body), so returning it re-compares two proven-equal values — the same one-function theater, moved onto the wire. And the feared \"one address, two canonicalisations\" was never possible: the Depot 400s any body that doesn't hash to its claimed address.\n\nSo: the unreachable TieredCas arms (put_tree, tree_entries, module section, test) are DELETED, and the genuine cross-binary comparison is installed where two independently-compiled binaries actually meet — the Depot's `PUT /v1/cas/trees/{hash}` re-serialises the parsed entries through its own linked `canonical_tree_bytes` and 400s with a distinct \"canonicalisation skew\" message on byte mismatch. Topology-proof: it guards the endpoint itself, so write-path reshuffles don't re-litigate it.\n\nThe load-bearing consequence is documented on `canonical_tree_bytes` and in ADR-0061 s8 (amended): the tree format may evolve ONLY by additive `Option` + `serde(default, skip_serializing_if)` — that is what makes parse→re-serialise byte-identical across one version of skew; a non-additive change fails mid-rollout PUTs closed, by design. Test: a body that hashes correctly and parses correctly but is not in canonical form is refused; its canonical twin is accepted.","files":null}]}